WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Hello, and welcome to this case study.

2
00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.040
My name is Iason, and I'll be your instructor.

3
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.800
So, what is a case study?

4
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.960
A case study allows you to apply your skills.

5
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:20.000
You won't learn any new concepts, but you'll have to combine the skills you've learned to solve a real-world problem.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.920
We recommend you taking some of these prerequisite courses before completing this case study.

7
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:33.240
In this case study, you'll work with a fictitious dataset from an online pet supply company called Whiskique.

8
00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.160
You have been hired as a new analyst to uncover insights into the

9
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:42.120
company's overall performance and possible improvement opportunities.

10
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.800
By the end of this course, you will successfully analyze an ecommerce dataset and

11
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:53.840
leverage your Power BI skills to create engaging dashboard-style pages full of insights.

12
00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:00.480
Firstly, we will examine some important terms and operations involved in ecommerce businesses.

13
00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.040
Let's look at an illustrative example of the total costs

14
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.600
for canned pet food through various stages in the supply chain.

15
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:15.840
In this example, for a $20 purchase of canned pet food, $4 goes to the manufacturer.

16
00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.560
This is also called the cost of goods sold or COGS.

17
00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.960
one dollar and 50 cents is spent on freight costs and fulfillment fees.

18
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:32.400
This includes the cost to ship a product from the manufacturer to the warehouse and the warehouse storage fees.

19
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.520
Last-mile delivery to customers costs $5.

20
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:42.400
This leaves a profit markup of $8 before administrative costs.

21
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.520
Whiskique pet supply has two primary business goals.

22
00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:51.440
The first is to serve as many customers as possible and increase sales.

23
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.680
The second is to reduce its operating expenses.

24
00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:03.480
Through the analysis work you will carry out for Whiskique, you will advise on increasing sales and reducing expenses.

25
00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:07.320
Let us begin with the opportunities for sales growth.

26
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:12.360
For sales growth, you will focus on upsell and cross-sell opportunities.

27
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:17.440
In a cross-sell, you promote a relevant product at the point of purchase.

28
00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:23.480
For example, when a customer is ready to buy a beach umbrella, present an offer on beach chairs.

29
00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:29.920
In an upsell, you promote a higher-priced alternative or a higher quantity of a product.

30
00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:36.040
For example, when a customer buys pet food regularly, present an offer on organic food.

31
00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:40.520
For shipping cost reductions, we can explore some of the following alternatives.

32
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.760
Consolidating multiple shipments into a single one.

33
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.000
Reducing package size dimensions and weight.

34
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.800
And, finally, shipping a higher quantity of a product.

35
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:58.360
A market basket analysis aims to find products frequently bought together in a single checkout.

36
00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:05.160
One way this is can be achieved is by determining the correlation coefficient between purchased quantities.

37
00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:10.120
This course will not cover this for Power BI, as it requires more advanced DAX formulas.

38
00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:16.400
However, you can identify which products are more regularly purchased with specific items.

39
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.240
We will be using a fictitious data source for product sales.

40
00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.840
The data is located in four CSV files.

41
00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.200
The sales table is the primary transaction table.

42
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.200
The product and customer tables contain additional attributes.

43
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.800
The state mapping has consistent values for locations.

44
00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:41.440
You will be creating relationships between the tables during your data preparation steps.

45
00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.520
With the basic information covered, you are now ready to

46
00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:49.240
analyze the data and help optimize Whiskique's business operations.

47
00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.520
Good luck!

